================================================================================
  SECTION 5: BUGS FOUND AND FIXED (v6.1 → v7.0)
================================================================================

  [BUG-10] HIGH: Ferdium not killed in saver mode
    PROBLEM: Ferdium.exe (Electron-based multi-messenger) was flagged in the
             May 24 energy report requesting 1ms timer resolution. This
             prevents deep CPU C-states and wastes significant power.
             5 Ferdium processes were running: ~600MB RAM, 179s CPU time.
             Not in any kill list in v6.1.
    FIX:     Added Ferdium.exe to saver kill list, re-apply kill list.
             Added KILL_FERDIUM=1 config toggle (like KILL_BROWSERS).
    IMPACT:  Battery was losing ~0.5-1W to Ferdium timer resolution requests.

  [BUG-11] MEDIUM: No duplicate mode detection
    PROBLEM: Log shows multiple cases of the same mode being activated twice
             in quick succession (e.g., Apr 29: Performance → Performance in
             44 seconds, May 13: 5 switches in 32 minutes).
             Each activation does full GPU enable/disable, service
             stop/start cycles unnecessarily.
    FIX:     Added CURRENT_MODE variable tracking. Each mode checks if already
             active and skips re-activation with a user-friendly message.
    IMPACT:  Eliminates redundant GPU cycles and service churn.

  [BUG-12] MEDIUM: WMIC deprecation
    PROBLEM: Script uses `wmic` throughout for battery status, CPU info,
             RAM usage, and uptime queries. WMIC is deprecated in
             Windows 11 24H2+ and produces deprecation warnings.
             May be removed entirely in future Windows builds.
    FIX:     Migrated all WMIC calls to PowerShell Get-CimInstance:
             - Menu battery status → CIM via temp files
             - Status dashboard (battery, CPU, RAM, uptime) → CIM
             - Battery health analysis → CIM + energy-report.html parsing
    IMPACT:  Future-proofed for Windows 11 24H2+, eliminates warnings.

  [BUG-13] MEDIUM: Ultra Performance has no battery protection
    PROBLEM: Log shows Ultra Performance used while on battery at least
             5 times. Battery life drops to ~1h30m in this mode.
             Battery health dropped from 88.8% to 85.8% in 41 days.
             No warning or confirmation was shown.
    FIX:     Added battery detection check before Ultra Performance.
             If on battery: shows warning with health stats, requires
             y/n confirmation. Logs whether user confirmed or declined.
    IMPACT:  Protects battery from accidental Ultra Perf drain.

  [BUG-14] MEDIUM: Service stop bottleneck (30+ seconds)
    PROBLEM: Sequential `net stop` commands for 13 services take 30-38
             seconds because each waits for the previous to complete.
             This delay occurs on every saver activation.
    FIX:     Replaced with PowerShell parallel jobs using Start-Job.
             All 13 services stop concurrently with 30-second timeout.
    IMPACT:  Expected to reduce service stop time from ~33s to ~8s.

  [BUG-15] LOW: Video quality not reset in balanced mode
    PROBLEM: Saver mode sets video to "power-save" (value=1).
             Ultra Performance sets to "max quality" (value=0).
             Balanced mode never reset video quality — it stayed at
             whatever the last mode set it to.
    FIX:     Added step [7/7] in balanced mode to reset video quality
             to default (value=0, "Optimize for Video Quality").
    IMPACT:  Video quality is now consistently set per mode.

  [BUG-16] LOW: PowerToys.PowerLauncher not killed
    PROBLEM: PowerToys.PowerLauncher.exe (Run utility) was not in the
             kill list despite other PowerToys modules being killed.
             Energy report showed it using 31.6s CPU time.
    FIX:     Added to kill list in saver mode and re-apply mode.
    IMPACT:  Minor — PowerLauncher uses less power than Awake but still
             wastes some CPU.

